WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Street Cop, Life on the Beat.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:25.620
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.800
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.320
And I'm Joe.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.720
And, you know, with a back of the box like that, this was really our chance for a DDR police

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.840
game on the NES.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.660
Remember, we're always talking about DDR games on the NES.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.160
This was the one time they had it.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.580
Life on the Beat.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.340
Yeah. I mean...

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.580
Oh, yeah. It could be all the police songs.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.260
Yeah. By the police.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:49.380
Tokyo Police Club.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.200
And... I like it better if it's just the police.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.860
Okay. Yeah. I was surprised you could actually just like pull out of out of thin air like another band

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.080
that also has police in there.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.200
Like you're on fire tonight, Joe.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.560
What can I say?

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.220
Okay. So here's the real deal, though.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.940
This is a Power Pad game as I was alluding to there.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.340
But also like the Power Pad, just like the Zapper in the episode before this, you know,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.360
we're getting these games and I guess it's not like such a big deal with the Zapper because that one pops up a little more frequently.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:34.280
But like this is weird now that the Power Pad was released in like 87 and had like maybe a game or two in 88 and is just still like casually just being dropped these games.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:43.880
But at this point, you know, since I got to imagine you're not really just like picking up a Power Pad and a copy of Street Cop like together at once, right?

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.000
It's a limited audience thing.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:59.620
These kinds of games, are they still worth it to just go Power Pad alone or should they start thinking about some kind of like dual functionality like we've been seeing with the Zapper games where it's like, yeah, you can own a Zapper or you can just use your NES controller.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:17.100
I feel like with a game like this, especially where they tried to get a bit more interactive with what you're doing, that this is the kind of game that you would do it with because this is probably the most ambitious Power Pad game we've played.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:25.220
Oh, for sure. But, you know, to the point of like, you know, the install base, you know, no one's buying a Power Pad anymore.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:38.960
I guess you could argue that because no one's buying a Power Pad anymore and because nobody's making Power Pad games for that reason, that the few people that are like, let's make a Power Pad game sort of corner that whole market of Power Pad owners.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.060
Like what are they going to do?

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.020
This is the only game that they have to buy.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.980
I mean, they're probably all going to buy it because they bought the Power Pad and they want to justify it.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:59.620
Well, I think the actual thing is Power Pad like diehards because I think by now probably 80% of the people that bought Power Pads have thrown them in a closet or in a basement and forgot they ever owned it.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:15.580
That's true. That is a good point though, Joe, because this is a Bandai game and Bandai were the ones who originally made the first version of the Power Pad, which was I think called like the Family Fitness something or, you know, one thing or another.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.320
And then like Nintendo was like, oh, that sounds great.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.740
Like, let's just buy the rights to that and license it as a Nintendo product.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:30.960
So it is mostly like the makers of the Power Pad being like, well, we made this thing, so we're just going to keep selling you games based off of it.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.640
Yeah. I mean, I guess that makes sense.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.620
And then, yeah, if no one else is stepping in, then people are probably going to buy it.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.140
Unless, like you said, Sean, people might have been turned off by the Power Pad already from the earlier games.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:55.200
Yeah. And I would also just like to clarify that when I say that this is the most ambitious Power Pad game we've ever seen, it's a very low bar to cross.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.140
Most of the games we've played have been the same game pretty much.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.140
So, but it is still very different from those.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:05.880
Yeah, that's true.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:10.320
That's true. We get, you know, we get like mostly mini games built into these Power Pads.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.220
Like there's never been like a complete game.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:21.840
It's been like, you know, you're at a camp and you can do all these different types of activities or you're doing gymnastics and here's your dance routine warm up and stuff like that.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:31.900
So to get an actual, you know, video game experience out of this, like, you know, where there's levels and you're constantly, you know, progressing.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:37.200
That is an interesting new addition to Power Pad games that we're seeing here in Street Cop.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:39.380
Yeah, for sure.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:47.640
But even Bandai doesn't really want you to play the game because in their own manual, they want you to do warm ups before you start playing.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.740
And they even go on to suggest maybe, you know, stretching out your knees and stuff, just making sure you're all ready to go.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.960
So that eats into your time playing the game.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.900
Then they also said, be sure that you're not bothering anybody else while you're playing.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:07.940
So if you're in the living room and your sister wants to do something else in the living room, like, sorry, kid, you got to wait till your sister's done because Bandai said so.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:10.840
Yeah, it sounds like the parents wrote this manual.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:14.920
Right. The last one, though, one hour a day.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:16.620
That's all you can play for.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:18.140
That's an appropriate playing time.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.680
Bandai wants to make sure you don't finish their game the same day you bought it.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.120
So just an hour a day, fans.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:30.240
I do appreciate just like because I'm a very easily annoyed person.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:35.780
So I appreciate that the makers of this game are just like, hey, maybe just stay out of people's way.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:44.380
Like, I think that like after like most like sound systems like in cars, they should they should like flash to to the driver.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.160
Like, hey, maybe turn the music down.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:50.780
Mario should come up and say like, hey, you've been playing the music too long.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.780
So this is a game where you play as a street cop.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:03.220
More specifically, I mean, in the in the Famicom version of this game, it was called Manhattan Police.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.280
So you're just like an NYPD police officer.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:06.900
Street cop sounds weird.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.460
I don't think there's like I don't think they get that kind of designation.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.040
Maybe somebody could tell me I'm wrong.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:14.220
And there are just people who are like, you're only ever going to be a street cop.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.400
You're the guy who just, you know, catches people on the streets.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.780
And then there's the indoor cops and they do different things.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:21.160
But I'm pretty sure that's not a real.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:28.900
I think it's like not a slang term, but I think it's like a layman's term for a like a beat cop.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:35.740
Yeah, I was going to say the same thing, which I do find interesting because in the manual it says that he's always wanted to be a famous detective.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.360
But I don't think a street cop is the same thing as the detective.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:39.740
Very much not.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:41.980
So so he failed.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.180
Yeah, he didn't achieve his dream.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:46.340
So he's dealing with life on the beat.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:52.060
And you as the player have to chase around these criminals that are wanted.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:56.200
You get like a wanted poster for each level of like trying to find these guys.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:58.340
But also you have to catch some other guys along the way.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:08.980
And then, you know, in terms of like what you're actually doing, you have to step on the buttons of the power pad corresponding to the cop's actions.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:12.340
So there's different buttons for walking.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:16.180
There's different buttons for turning, jumping and clubbing.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:23.180
Using your club is a power pad action, which I guess you could club with your foot in that regard.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:26.540
But on screen, the cop is only ever going to do it with his hands.

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:28.880
And that's the general gist of the game.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.820
You are tracking down these criminals in your game.

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:38.900
But you're you're almost like we need to talk about how you move as the police officer, because that's the challenge of the game.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:47.820
Right. Is you being the power pad, translating that to the character and then therefore trying to use that character to catch these criminals.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:56.480
Because if you're using it like a regular controller, catching these criminals would just be making sure that you're holding the D pad in the same position that they're heading.

00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:59.660
But because you have the power pad, you have to play things a little differently.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:09.420
Yeah. So basically, like any power pad game, you have a standing position and that's like the middle two buttons.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:18.680
I forgot which which specifically and that and just, you know, oscillating between those two that makes you walk.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:25.860
But then you've got these other buttons that like one is your turn button or two are your turn buttons.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:33.820
And you have to like use those in conjunction with your standing buttons so that like you're actually turning and then you return to your standing position.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:39.900
And then you've got your sidestep buttons with which are to each side of your standing buttons.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:47.220
And then your action and club buttons are often the corners, even though you're doing that action with your hands.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:52.260
You still have to like step into an awkward spot to like use your club.

00:08:54.340 --> 00:09:06.120
So that's what I meant in the earlier segment where like this would make more sense if maybe your controller was used as well,

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:13.240
because as complicated as the as the actual movement keys are on the power pad,

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:21.040
it wouldn't be that much of a stretch if the things that you would normally be using your hands for were on the controller.

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:24.240
Well, you can use the controller for the club.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.420
I don't know if it's made clear in the manual, but you have the option.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.540
Yeah. So I and I was thinking that, too.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:32.420
I was like, that should just be the way it is.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:37.000
Like there should be a weird button you have to step on to use the object in your hand.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:44.100
But but yeah, I think that the personally I think that the best way to do it is also having using the controller for the clubbing.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:50.060
Yeah. But then you have to think about like the you know, not that they had short cords on these things,

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:56.100
but, you know, holding the pad and moving around the power moving around on the power pad.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:02.960
I could imagine that could be some recipe for disaster for certain living rooms where you're getting like tangled up in your power pad.

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:08.680
Yeah, I mean, I'm sure it happens, but it doesn't really ever require you to like move much.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:12.520
I mean, you're pretty much stay in the center of the power pad and you'll use one foot to tap.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:14.480
If you need to jump or if you have to jump.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:15.880
But if you have to jump, you still just jump.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:20.120
You stay in one spot unless you jump like so high that you that you rip the NES out of the wall.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:21.060
Yeah, that's what I put.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:22.940
That's the vision I have in my head now, I guess.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:31.580
But, you know, I was kind of impressed with with a couple of the little things like there were a couple of things when I was thinking about it.

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:32.520
I was like, well, this is dumb.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:34.700
I'm like, it's going to be it's going to be awkward to.

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:37.060
Oh, you have to reach over.

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:41.700
You have to press the button in front of you diagonally to turn to the right.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:47.580
It just feels so unintuitive, you know, instead of like stepping to the side to the right side to turn to the right.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:11:04.420
But what I learned was, you know, in practice, this is going to sound like really dumb, but you you take your left foot and you kind of cross it over to in front of your right foot to press the button that it requires you to turn to the right.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:09.980
And if you think about that motion, that is like one half step of turning to the right.

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:13.780
Like, yeah, like I felt like maybe that maybe that's obvious.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:14.920
It wasn't obvious.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.560
I was like, I think it's obvious when you have a power.

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.480
Yeah, yeah, probably.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:21.260
I understand what you're saying there, Joe.

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:24.400
And and I think like the manual actually is its own worst enemy.

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:38.140
And it's the kind of game that like is just better off like testing out on your own, you know, using that using the power pad and seeing how the movement is done, because the manual goes through the process of like assigning everything to numbers.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:44.000
And has this like weird way of even just on the on the page about how to operate the power pad.

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:55.360
It has this like incomprehensible like it looks like alien language to me of how to turn corners of like beyond six and seven, press three, go back to six and seven, press two.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:58.300
It's like and it just continues to do that, though.

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:02.220
It's like, you know, to make Ben change directions, step on 13 and 11.

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:04.820
It's like, don't don't explain it like that.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:08.000
Like there's got to be the best is the little diagram.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:17.980
It has on that page that has seven, the three, another six and another seven and just arrows pointing from like the six to the three and the seven to the two.

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:19.620
And I'm just like, what is this telling?

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:29.460
Yeah, you take the little sections of this like 12 button pad out of context and put them in weird arrangements.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:31.620
Yeah, that's definitely clear.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:35.040
And it's also instructions that are stacked on top of each other.

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:36.440
So it looks like one thing.

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:38.100
Yeah, it's two separate things.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.340
And there's also like the full diagram of the pad.

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:45.300
That's also a diagram of plugging it into the NES.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:48.600
So you've got like, oh, use this button to turn left.

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.680
Plug the NES into the TV.

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:52.860
Like, what are you doing?

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:58.580
Yeah, I love how it's got like it's got like a like a line that describes what each button is for.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:02.180
And there's also got a line that just says like NES, like this is the NES.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:03.560
This is the plug.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.980
It's like, yeah, we know what those things are already.

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:07.120
Explain the power pad.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:09.480
Right. And I'm not sure.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:15.120
I'm not sure if they could, because I don't want to like be presumptuous about their intention here.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:19.060
But again, like Joe said, the game isn't like the movement isn't unbearable.

00:13:19.220 --> 00:13:24.720
It's not like turning is this four button input thing that is like impossible to do.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:35.240
However, you got to feel like they were trying to make it like the fact that, you know, you can't just walk so easily in this game is half the appeal of the game.

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:42.360
Right. It's like thinking about modern day quap where you're on track and field and you can only use Q, W, O and P to move your body.

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:46.700
I feel like in a way that is kind of the appeal here.

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:50.800
If you were able to just be like, you know, marching on six and seven.

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:56.820
And then if you start marching on six and two, the character turns to the left now and he walks that way.

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:59.360
And then you have to go back to six and seven to walk straight.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:02.420
It's like theoretically that could have worked.

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:04.020
Right. Like they could have assigned more.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:05.520
I feel like that'd be a nightmare.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:08.640
No. Yeah. But I'm saying like that would have been like a real body thing.

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:18.680
However, I feel like that loses the appeal of the game, which is that like sometimes you're moving really fast as your cop and you're like gaining speed on the person you're trying to capture.

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:25.720
And other times you like get caught up in like the turning of directions or the movement of your character and you're walking really slow.

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:28.380
And it's like, I'm not in control of the cop.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.660
I think it takes a little bit of time.

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.560
I think any comparison to quap, like obviously that's not intentional.

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:39.900
Like we like that now because it's sort of it.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:49.560
I don't think that they had that level of of of self self awareness that like, oh, wow, this is kind of a ridiculous game to control.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:51.160
But isn't that part of the fun?

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:56.300
Like, no, I think they were trying to make this the most comfortable, comfortable experiences they could.

00:14:57.160 --> 00:15:00.800
And quap is sort of a modern a modern thing.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:08.840
Yeah. Also, I want to point out that if you were to march on six and two after having turned, you would no longer be facing the television.

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:10.760
And then it'd be worse if you turned again.

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:12.640
You did like your back and be turned to the television.

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:15.880
Right. I think that's like a hurdle that they couldn't overcome.

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:32.780
Yeah. Sure. But I guess, you know, as as intuitive as I'm trying to make it sound, it is also just like it at that point, Sean, it is just better to not play this game with a power pad because like definitely controlling the cop isn't a smooth experience with the power pad.

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:37.320
Like, I guess the bigger issue is that they didn't make Walking Impossible on the power pad.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:48.300
But, you know, if if the if they were trying to make a smooth experience of being the cop and everything, I guess like, you know, it's a double edged sword because they're Bandai and they're trying to make a game for the power pad.

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:55.440
But at a certain point, it'd be better just experienced on a controller if they're just going for the smooth experience.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:58.600
Yeah, I get that.

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:03.720
But like this game is so designed around the power pad that I am.

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:18.480
You know, I think that this game proves to me something that none of the other power pad games have yet that which is that there is any reason to make another game on the power pad like like this game is designed around the power pad.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:28.720
I think the point the primary point is to have something to do on the power pad and the secondary point is to like be this to like the rest of the game is like the secondary point.

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:36.660
Like, I don't think that this game if you were to make this game for a controller, then you might as well kind of throw out all the mechanics and make a game that makes more sense in the controller.

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:38.860
You know what I mean? Yeah, that's a good point.

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:49.060
That's a good point. Yeah, the game as it stands, if you were to just put it on a D-pad would be laughably easy and you'd be done in 15 minutes.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:54.820
Right. Because the objective here of, you know, tracking down these enemies and stuff like that.

00:16:55.420 --> 00:17:01.960
They do give you a map that you can see your position and the enemy's positions and stuff like that.

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:05.520
But these aren't like crazy mazes or anything like that.

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:09.480
Some of them lead to dead ends and stuff that could work for or against you.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:19.360
But for the most part, it's about being able to keep up with your enemies and turn corners, you know, quickly and stuff like that.

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:25.680
But I would say, if anything, that's the interesting part of the game, though, right?

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:35.660
As you go on in the stages, the levels and the mazes become a little more intricate in the amount of, like, turns and where you can go and how you can explore.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:41.760
You know, I don't know if, like, we want to talk about if that's, like, a complete enough experience.

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:46.440
Just chasing down enemies, is that enough of a game or not?

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:52.000
I think for a power pad game, yeah, because that's more game than we've ever had.

00:17:52.460 --> 00:18:03.580
Every other thing that came out for this peripheral has been run fast or jump high or a combination of the two, because that's really all you can do.

00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:07.900
At least this has some context to it, aside from a competition.

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:10.080
Yeah, I was going to say pretty much the same thing.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:16.980
I think that if you were a power pad owner in 1989, then, yes, this is definitely, like, enough.

00:18:17.260 --> 00:18:25.000
At least it feels like enough, maybe because of the illusion of having so little from all the other games, that this feels like, wow, this is a full experience.

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:31.700
I don't know that that's necessarily true today or even, like, probably a couple years later when people look back on this game.

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:40.160
But, like, at the time, I feel like it almost feels like they went above and beyond compared to other developers or other games, rather.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:49.980
It just feels a little bare at times, though, because you'll have, you know, in later levels, you have, like, things you have to jump over, right?

00:18:50.180 --> 00:18:51.220
Obstacles in your way.

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:53.480
And it's sparse, though.

00:18:53.620 --> 00:19:09.060
There are some, actually many, rooms in this game when you're exploring that, like, theoretically, if the enemy wasn't spawned there, nothing is happening on that particular, you know, part of the level or in those rooms when you start navigating the rooms and stuff like that.

00:19:09.060 --> 00:19:10.400
They're just very bare.

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:18.000
And I wonder if there was a way that they could have made this more of a video game.

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:21.080
You know, we're saying, like, hey, this is definitely the most game we've gotten out of the Power Pad.

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:37.900
But if they made it more of a platformer in the sense of, like, you know, walking and jumping and controlling all of that, if they built that more into the stages, if, like, the first half was running around these mazes and then the second half with the boss was more of a platforming experiment.

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:40.960
Rather than just a continuation of the first half.

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:44.040
Yeah, I agree now that you mention that.

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:47.640
I think that, like, there was some, like, oh, this evolves.

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:51.140
Now there's, like, some jumping and now there's, like, you know, these items you find.

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:55.440
But, yeah, I think it all happened so spaced out and so slowly that the whole game feels kind of empty.

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:59.060
You're right. It would have been nice to have, like, I don't know, a little more.

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:13.040
I don't know if it needed, like, more, like, platforming or something like that because the input method and the space that you're occupying in this game is already kind of novel.

00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:23.320
And it's a bit more difficult when you're actually playing it than you would with a controller.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:25.940
So I don't know if it needed more obstacles.

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:34.940
I think that would create much more frustration than it would more fun because your input is already kind of a challenge.

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:48.140
Well, but, you know, if you're being, like, Super Mario, right, like on the power pad, most of your inputting is just going to be stepping on the same, you know, six and seven over and over again as if you're marching in place.

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:54.160
And then just actually jumping when you build up enough momentum to jump to the next platform or something like that.

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:56.420
I'm not saying they should have made this a Super Mario Brothers game.

00:20:56.420 --> 00:21:07.340
But I think, like, that kind of thing, you know, yes, it could get messy if they required you to do, like, a lot of quick turns or also using items at the same time or clubbing action at the same time.

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:17.740
But I think just the idea of a game where you're running and there's more peril in your way could have been fun if they had, you know, designed it as such.

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:20.220
Not if they just, like, randomly placed that throughout the levels.

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:30.900
But if they actually, like, designed some platforming challenges, I'm not just talking about, like, you know, jumping over roofs or something, you know, just the idea of some form of running and jumping.

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:32.760
That's, like, the NES is bread and butter.

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:35.300
So it's, like, I think that could have worked well on a power pad.

00:21:35.300 --> 00:21:50.820
Yeah, I just think that, like, if you had any more precision necessary to play this game correctly, I think that would just be a recipe for disaster with how frustrating it could be.

00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:59.120
I was just going to suggest that even if it's not, like, precision-based jumping, even if it's, like, okay, there's extra time bonuses up above you in some levels, you just jump.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:02.460
Just to give you a, like, I know it sounds dumb, but, like, just to give you a reason to jump.

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:05.260
You have this power pad and it gives you the option to jump.

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:08.660
I mean, you know, just to give you a reason to, like, do something else.

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:14.380
Collect these things and they'll, you know, they'll give you, like, a bonus or they'll give you an extra item or something.

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:17.500
Or, you know, it doesn't, and I think we're, like, hung up on jumping.

00:22:17.500 --> 00:22:27.780
But I think, like, what, you know, what I was sort of agreeing with was that it does, after a while, the game does feel kind of repetitive once the novelty of the power pad stuff wears off.

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:37.660
So, like, yeah, I do think it would be nice to have, like, something else going on by, like, later stages instead of just kind of similar things with, like, a more complex maze.

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:48.360
But, yeah, like, I agree that if it's, like, if you're doing, like, actual Mario-esque jumps, like, jump directly on this Koopa or don't jump down this hole, that would get really frustrating.

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:56.960
But if it's just something that, like, gives you another reason to do this activity that the power pad is, like, supposed to make you do, it would be nice.

00:22:56.960 --> 00:23:05.220
Yeah, Bandai even positions it as, like, it takes your entire body to operate the power pad, just like a sport.

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:12.060
And with our Street Cop, you can exercise both your brain and body while you enjoy yourself playing.

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:15.320
And so I think you're on the right page there, Sean.

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:23.900
Even, like, what Bandai was thinking of, like, these games don't have to be that hard because the enjoyment of them is that your body is the controller.

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:25.700
Like, that's the novelty of it, right?

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:30.720
I think that's the novelty and the, like, the difficulty curve.

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:40.520
I think if you're not, I think this is not the same kind of challenge that we usually get from a video game.

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:51.400
Like, you're going to, if you play this game for long enough, you're going to be out of breath and you're not going to be fast enough sometimes or agile enough to get to your guy.

00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:56.200
So it's sort of, the challenge is built into your body.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:24.780
So other than the, like, I guess, normal street criminals that you find in this game, each, catching enough of those criminals eventually leads up to taking down the guy who was wanted in that particular stage.

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:31.760
So whether you're at the park or the warehouse or specifically Harlem in New York City.

00:24:32.220 --> 00:24:34.160
Yeah. I don't know why that's a thing.

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:40.380
But, like, so if you're at any of these stages, like, eventually it says, like, you know, this is the guy who's wanted.

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:55.640
And that's, like, this is where the game, I guess, gets a little more interesting because they have a specific quirk to them and a thing that, you know, whether it's an item or a type of strategy that you'll need in order to take them down successfully.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:57.780
I don't know if you guys want to talk about these guys.

00:24:57.900 --> 00:24:59.960
We don't have to go about each of them, like, in depth or anything.

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:08.940
But this was some nice flavor to an otherwise kind of bland game of everybody just looking the same or not really standing out.

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:11.980
These enemies, these bosses do stand out.

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:16.320
Yeah, they give you, like, a little goal to, like, go towards, like, oh, like, there's the guy I saw at the beginning.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:17.540
There's a little cut scene at the beginning.

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:19.160
Or are they all purse snatchers?

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:21.900
They all, like, punch someone and steal something and run away.

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:28.720
But you see them at the beginning of each level and then it's satisfying to see them at the end and kind of feel like it's like a boss fight.

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:36.740
Yeah. They have some really goofball energy in their descriptions.

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:41.080
Like, I think my favorite is Bloody Betty.

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:43.800
And I'm just going to read it word for word from the manual.

00:25:45.380 --> 00:25:46.280
She's 19.

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:51.180
She's a former female gang leader, now a habitual extorter.

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:52.800
Good at knife throwing.

00:25:53.380 --> 00:25:56.680
Initially, Betty was a good kid at a poverty stricken family.

00:25:57.420 --> 00:25:58.260
I'm sorry.

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:04.940
But a poverty stricken family environment and a cruel world have turned her into a cold hearted person.

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:09.060
She has a habit of saying it's all because of poverty.

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:11.260
And I just.

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:15.380
OK, first of all, like, that's that's that's so fucking stupid.

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:22.500
But I just love the image of my in my head of, like, somebody committing a crime, like stealing my wallet.

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:25.000
And then as they run away, just like, ha ha.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:26.460
It's all because of poverty.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:31.720
That's a very Eric Andre thing to have happen to you in New York City.

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:35.260
Yeah. I liked Big Burger.

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:37.320
He is an eat and run offender.

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:39.160
That's what that's why you're chasing him down.

00:26:39.340 --> 00:26:43.360
He has he has committed too many dine and dashes, I think is what we would call them.

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:46.920
But they're making it sound a little more deadly with eat and run.

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:59.900
But I think, like, you know, when you get to stage four, right, and you're at the construction site and you're taking down Big Burger at that point, like, if we're just taking it, we're still just taking down guys who have dined and dashed.

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:02.260
Like, how serious is this cop?

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:05.160
Like, he has nothing better to do with his time.

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:12.240
Like, I guess former female gang leader at age 19, which assumes that she was a gang leader, you know, when she was 16 or something.

00:27:12.700 --> 00:27:14.120
Specifically a female gang leader.

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:15.280
You have to keep that in mind.

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:18.260
Well, yeah, because back then they wouldn't let the gangs mix.

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:20.540
You were either a male gang or a female gang.

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:21.840
Wait a minute. Okay.

00:27:21.880 --> 00:27:25.980
So she was the leader of a female gang or she was the gang leader who happens to be female?

00:27:27.040 --> 00:27:28.980
Hmm. We should look into this.

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:36.340
I don't think you would ever have to specify the gender of the person if, you know, like a former female gang leader.

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:37.980
Yeah, but it's 1989, you know.

00:27:38.140 --> 00:27:41.040
They're like, oh, they're like, yeah, you know, they're like, they're just not as progressive.

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:43.040
Got it. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:44.240
They're like making it known.

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:45.980
They're like a little girl gang leader.

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:52.480
Yeah. Which, again, would still beg the question, is she leading a gang of little girls or not?

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:54.480
I think we don't have all the information.

00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:58.840
The English language is not complex enough to give us this information.

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:09.240
Right. And then it all, of course, it all builds up to taking down Don Mayone cheese or Maywan cheese or something.

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:11.600
I have no idea what they're trying to do with that.

00:28:11.780 --> 00:28:13.420
Like, are they trying to make it sound Italian?

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:14.920
Because it doesn't sound Italian.

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:16.320
Yeah. I'm thinking. Yeah.

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:20.700
I thought it was trying to go with like something with some pun on like mayonnaise or something,

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:22.180
but then with cheese in there.

00:28:22.340 --> 00:28:24.720
And I guess cheese is on some Italian.

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:25.960
Don Mayone cheese.

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:28.500
Mayone cheese. There you go.

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:34.960
Mayone cheese. And he's really the second boss of the Mayone cheese mafia family.

00:28:35.220 --> 00:28:38.980
And he's a coward who hates anything and everything painful.

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:41.060
Even if it's just.

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:42.380
If it's just an inoculation.

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:46.640
That is so specific.

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:48.520
He's been quoted as saying as such.

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:49.500
He says, you know what?

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:51.920
I just hate when things are painful.

00:28:52.380 --> 00:28:54.440
And, you know, even if it's just an inoculation.

00:28:54.980 --> 00:28:56.120
Here's the thing, though.

00:28:56.420 --> 00:28:59.840
Okay. He's the second boss of the mafia family.

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:01.580
Is that implying that he's not top dog?

00:29:01.640 --> 00:29:04.380
Or is he just like the second in the line?

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:08.120
Another unclear aspect of the English language.

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:09.960
There's no way for us to know.

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:12.640
See, I took it as he's.

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:14.480
Yeah, I didn't think of it the other way.

00:29:14.540 --> 00:29:15.600
But I took it as he's the.

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:17.520
Like, he's the number two in command.

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:19.020
And I was like, wow, this is like.

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:20.520
He's like a consigliere.

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:25.140
They said like that the cop, the main character.

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:28.100
Was like just out of the police academy.

00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:30.340
So I was like, are they really sticking to be like that realistic?

00:29:30.520 --> 00:29:35.940
Well, a rookie cop would never take down the actual Don of the whatever.

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:37.080
The mob.

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:40.300
Well, I feel like his name is actually just Don.

00:29:41.500 --> 00:29:44.920
Yeah. And not the Don.

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:48.240
But then again, you know, just impossible to tell.

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:49.220
And the mafia family.

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:50.620
That's just their last name is mafia.

00:29:50.920 --> 00:29:52.200
He's just the boss.

00:29:55.060 --> 00:29:57.880
Don man cheese, which again, we still don't know if we're saying that.

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:08.820
Right. He has an interesting like quirk about his final boss fight, though, where I feel like he doesn't really like leave the the middle plane of battlefield.

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:17.180
So like you can just kind of like, you know, walk out and and be on the other sides of the room.

00:30:17.180 --> 00:30:25.740
And like he can't understand that concept of like you're on the far side of the room and he's still just hanging out in the middle, pacing back and forth.

00:30:26.380 --> 00:30:30.140
Granted, the second you join him in the middle, he's ready to shoot at you.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:31.500
So I guess that's worth something.

00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:39.680
I just thought that was interesting that he wasn't really familiar with the idea of like walking around rooms just immediately in the middle of them.

00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:43.840
Well, he's not going to get close to you because he's too cowardly to do so.

00:30:44.060 --> 00:30:46.800
But that would also imply that he would try and get away from you.

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:58.080
So who knows? Also, with this game's protagonist being all about walking and running and movement, I think that makes Don man cheese a perfect foil to the protagonist.

00:30:58.260 --> 00:31:03.480
He doesn't move at all. That is so funny and so true.

00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:07.420
And I think Bandai was, you know, making some commentary there.

00:31:08.720 --> 00:31:10.820
Right. Yeah. It's really well written.

00:31:11.100 --> 00:31:20.900
He has no fear of dogs either, which is important to note, because even though that seems like just some color commentary, you do have like a dog that you can call to in this game.

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:24.800
And that will do nothing for you against Don man cheese.

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:33.240
I wish that I could just describe myself in like just non sequitur things that I'm not like.

00:31:34.280 --> 00:31:37.200
I'm specifically not into lettuce.

00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:41.680
I don't know that that one didn't hit.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:43.480
But you know what I'm saying?

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:45.540
I like it. No, I get you.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:51.280
I mean, like all these people are describing themselves strangely because you even have Speedy Lewis over in Harlem.

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:57.100
And he his hands can move so fast that he's convinced robbery is his life's calling.

00:31:57.300 --> 00:32:00.120
It's like that's what he decides to do with fast fingers.

00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:03.440
Like he could be an amazing secretary.

00:32:04.440 --> 00:32:05.300
That's true.

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:08.380
Making an honest living.

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:11.060
But that is poverty for you.

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:13.840
It's all because of it.

00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:21.780
I briefly mentioned the dog and that made me realize that we haven't talked about items at all.

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:26.400
Not that they're like, you know, they're not the most important thing in this game.

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:36.820
But they are a thing that you have to use to track down certain wanted characters or just to use against them to have a better shot for them.

00:32:37.240 --> 00:32:39.320
So guys, the items in this game.

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:42.080
It's weird how you find them though too, right?

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:45.700
Like not that this game was grounded in realism or anything.

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:50.000
But taking down enemies and then just having like the items appear felt a little cheap.

00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:57.500
Again, I felt like this was a moment where you could have like stumbled across them in the environment where you have to like open a box or something.

00:32:57.540 --> 00:33:05.680
Or you go inside the buildings and there's like, you know, instead of all these empty rooms in these buildings, there's meaningful content in these rooms where you can find these items.

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:10.380
But there are some things where it's like you go into one room and you find an item in there.

00:33:10.440 --> 00:33:15.520
Like the orange is in the warehouse, which is where, you know, that's where oranges are.

00:33:15.780 --> 00:33:19.540
Well, that's the, yeah, that's the Tropicana orange warehouse.

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:20.880
That's what it is. Yeah.

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:25.020
You can find like, you can find mustard or something in a restaurant.

00:33:25.020 --> 00:33:27.320
I think that kind of makes sense.

00:33:27.800 --> 00:33:29.560
But yeah, some of them don't make sense.

00:33:29.680 --> 00:33:36.960
Like, I don't know what was going on at this police academy, but you, you're the cop.

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:41.800
You can just like throw firecrackers at people.

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:44.460
And I feel like that's not really protocol.

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:47.980
And then you have like, you can throw cans of Coke specifically.

00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:51.220
But also their bombs.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:55.620
Like, I don't know if that's, if that's legal in the NYPD specifically.

00:33:56.180 --> 00:34:00.000
Yeah. I don't know if you're allowed to use bombs, but the enemies have no problems using them on you too.

00:34:00.140 --> 00:34:01.380
So I guess it's fair game, right?

00:34:01.380 --> 00:34:03.880
It's like, oh, if he's going to throw a bomb at me, I'm going to throw a bomb right back.

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:09.860
Well, it also says like, I think somewhere in the manual, I can't find it right now, but.

00:34:10.220 --> 00:34:16.420
Oh yeah, here. Oh no, there's somewhere in the manual where it says, oh, throw a bomb to, to knock down the enemy.

00:34:16.880 --> 00:34:20.600
And there's like something else, like shoot the bullet to, to slow down the enemy.

00:34:20.720 --> 00:34:23.020
Like those aren't what bullets and bombs do.

00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:24.600
I mean, it does slow people down.

00:34:25.240 --> 00:34:28.040
That's true. Actually, they're just not being super specific.

00:34:28.420 --> 00:34:31.700
Yeah. In a 2D NES game, can we really tell where that bullet went?

00:34:31.900 --> 00:34:40.340
For all we know, you're shooting them in like, you know, the, the meatiest part of their leg where it just like knocks them down, but they, but they won't die from it.

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:41.860
You know, they just suffer complications.

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:43.720
Yeah. Complications.

00:34:44.180 --> 00:34:45.440
People die from complications.

00:34:45.880 --> 00:34:47.420
Anyway, more items in this game.

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:53.540
I mean, you know, like the, I don't know what else to say here, but like, you know, it's a colorful assortment.

00:34:53.720 --> 00:34:57.100
Like Sean was saying about the Coke can and stuff like that, or, or the mustard.

00:34:57.420 --> 00:35:04.700
And, you know, these, these are like, I guess these items make somewhat sense when you think about like the stages you find them in.

00:35:04.840 --> 00:35:08.980
Like stage four is where the mustard is and that's where a burger bill is and stuff.

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:15.300
So like, I get it, but it's just a, it's an odd assortment of, of power ups, if you will.

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:17.720
And I found that they're not even really necessary.

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:20.240
Yeah. A lot of them.

00:35:20.420 --> 00:35:22.260
Like you could just use your club for most things.

00:35:24.100 --> 00:35:30.720
Yeah. There are some that are like built in to be necessary, but yeah, the other ones like, I mean, the bullet is nice.

00:35:30.860 --> 00:35:38.220
I mean, it would be nice to have a gun, I guess too, but, but yeah, you just get a bullet and, you know, I find it interesting how generic all of the items are.

00:35:38.320 --> 00:35:42.860
You just got an orange or a bullet or mustard or the VMAX turbo drink.

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:45.920
Just all generic things you find lying around your house.

00:35:46.140 --> 00:35:56.880
Right. But you, you know, you don't need the bomb to stop Speedy Lewis, but you might want the VMAX turbo drink since that lets you like run faster to keep up with him.

00:35:57.060 --> 00:35:59.480
Like, I get that, you know?

00:36:00.280 --> 00:36:09.820
So, so the items, it's almost in a way to help with Sean's point earlier of like not making this game any more complicated than just your body being the inputs.

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:17.960
It's, it's nice that the items aren't like mandatory and like you have to perfectly time a thrown bomb in order to proceed with taking down Speedy Lewis.

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:20.020
Like, it's like, if you do it, great.

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:21.240
It makes the fight a little easier.

00:36:21.440 --> 00:36:22.320
If you don't, no sweat.

00:36:22.400 --> 00:36:24.140
You got your baton. You'll beat the shit out of him.

00:36:25.280 --> 00:36:28.280
Yeah. You know, I'm looking at the manual.

00:36:28.420 --> 00:36:33.680
Can I just point out one more thing that I find interesting in the way that they made this manual with, for the items specifically?

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:42.640
Sure. Is that, uh, so if you look on the page with the items, there's the first column has four items and it fills up the whole page.

00:36:42.880 --> 00:36:47.700
But the second and third column only have three items, which leaves two empty spots below there.

00:36:47.940 --> 00:36:55.720
But instead of putting the two other items in the game below there, they put one paragraph that encompasses, that covers both of those two empty spots that just says,

00:36:55.840 --> 00:36:59.280
there are other items in the game that increase Ben's power and lengthen the time.

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:00.740
But they don't tell you what it is.

00:37:00.860 --> 00:37:03.700
And there's two spots right there that can just tell you what those items are.

00:37:03.700 --> 00:37:06.620
Maybe they wrote this before they finished the game.

00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:09.520
Maybe. And they're like, we don't know what those are going to be.

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:15.220
It just feels very like, it feels very like Gilligan's Island and the rest for me when there's only two people left.

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:18.460
And you took up the space that you would put the two items.

00:37:18.640 --> 00:37:20.720
Might as well just use that space to tell us what those items are.

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:26.920
Well, they're also using asterisks for like bullet point lists here, which I get.

00:37:27.060 --> 00:37:30.620
And then all of a sudden it's not formatted with the rest of the bullets.

00:37:30.620 --> 00:37:32.980
It says that thing about there are other items.

00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:36.980
And that just makes that look like a reference note for an asterisk.

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:38.440
Yeah, it looks like it's supposed to be a footnote.

00:37:38.580 --> 00:37:42.920
Right. Yeah. So then you go back up and you're like, oh, this is a bullet.

00:37:43.180 --> 00:37:45.160
But are there other bullets?

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:47.720
Is that why the asterisk is also next to that one?

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:49.880
Like there are other bullets in this game?

00:37:50.040 --> 00:37:52.260
There are other, like keys, if you will.

00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:53.840
Yeah. There are bullets in this game.

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:59.240
Every item has another form that increases Ben's power and one that lengthens the time available for Ben.

00:37:59.620 --> 00:38:03.440
Followed up on the next page by playing too much is not good for your health.

00:38:03.740 --> 00:38:08.300
In all capitals, bold print, largest print I've seen on any page so far.

00:38:08.540 --> 00:38:12.760
They really went above and beyond here to make sure that they didn't get sued in the United States.

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:15.240
Yeah. Hey, we're getting to the nitty gritty.

00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:20.140
Above that, it says be sure to do knee bends, jumps, and so on.

00:38:20.600 --> 00:38:24.240
Is that like, is there a common order we should all know after knee bends and jumps?

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:25.180
Like what's next?

00:38:25.340 --> 00:38:30.380
Well, there are other warm-ups that can increase your power or length in your time of playing the game.

00:38:30.880 --> 00:38:32.620
Well, on top of all that. They could have told us what those were.

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:35.360
On top of all that, too. I'm not done with knee bends and jumps.

00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:39.900
Okay. My theory is that it's knee bends, jumps, and then lands.

00:38:40.620 --> 00:38:44.320
Because you do the knee bend, next you do the jump, and what do you do once you're in the air?

00:38:44.400 --> 00:38:45.260
The next thing you do is land.

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:46.880
You do do that. So that's what they didn't tell you.

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:58.120
Well, my thing is that like if this weren't a complicated enough manual already, it then says this equipment generates and uses radio frequency energy and is not installed or used properly.

00:38:58.120 --> 00:39:04.960
That is, in strict accordance with the FCC, manufacturing instructions may cause interference to radio and television reception.

00:39:06.260 --> 00:39:08.920
Wow. That is, I've actually never read that.

00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:11.920
Yeah. That actually sounds very sci-fi to me.

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:13.740
It's on all of them.

00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:16.320
Don't worry about it. I just thought we should read the entire manual.

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:21.540
But I've never actually read the compliance with FCC regulations.

00:39:22.260 --> 00:39:25.800
This might be the best example of a manual that's more fun than the game.

00:39:26.600 --> 00:39:31.580
Like, you know, like when you get the joy out of reading a manual, we really dug through the manual.

00:39:31.900 --> 00:39:36.920
In terms of the actual game, it's like, it's the very first walking simulator.

00:39:37.040 --> 00:39:38.120
It's a walking simulator.

00:39:39.820 --> 00:39:42.380
God. It's so funny how we do this like modern.

00:39:42.400 --> 00:39:43.600
That's true. Right.

00:39:43.780 --> 00:39:45.580
But we do this with modern things all the time.

00:39:45.700 --> 00:39:47.000
Like Sean said earlier with QWOP.

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:48.680
It's like they didn't know about QWOP.

00:39:48.760 --> 00:39:50.180
They weren't trying to be QWOP.

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:53.200
Like, stop making QWOP like a comparison.

00:39:53.460 --> 00:39:54.820
It's the same thing with all of these things.

00:39:54.920 --> 00:39:58.440
It's so funny because at the end of the day, they were just trying to make money.

00:39:58.740 --> 00:39:59.720
That's all they cared about.

00:39:59.720 --> 00:40:05.020
They were just trying to sell a game for children to buy because they happened to already buy their power pad.

00:40:05.860 --> 00:40:08.200
No, I think this is someone's passion project.

00:40:10.280 --> 00:40:14.400
You've made me, like, just view this podcast in a completely different way.

00:40:14.400 --> 00:40:17.160
I feel like I'm being taken advantage of now.

00:40:17.380 --> 00:40:28.400
Well, if you think that your time is being taken advantage of, Sean, wait till you hear us take advantage of the listener's time where we continue to talk about Street Cop in a little thing we call the Essential Games List.

00:40:31.180 --> 00:40:37.380
Joe, you've had a lot to say, so I want you to be the first to say whether Street Cop is an essential game or not.

00:40:38.560 --> 00:40:50.500
Well, I did mention that I think that this is the first power pad game that, like, somewhat, a little bit to me, justifies the existence of the power pad.

00:40:50.700 --> 00:40:56.640
You know, it feels like it's a full experience that uses the power pad in a way that I'm like, oh, okay, I get it.

00:40:56.640 --> 00:41:04.760
Like, I'm playing as the cop, and it's like these little archaic movements that sort of translate, and it works in that sense.

00:41:04.900 --> 00:41:19.060
And I'll also say that I'm, you know, I always like seeing something that tries something new or, you know, this has, you know, it has a cute little story, and it has a little bit of, like, you know, different environments and interesting things.

00:41:19.160 --> 00:41:25.440
So in that sense, like, it's not, I think it's the best power pad game we've played.

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:32.940
And I think that if you have a power pad, and you're itching to play it, definitely pick up this game.

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:38.260
For any other reason, though, like, there's no, don't go seeking out a power pad.

00:41:38.360 --> 00:41:39.240
There's no reason to get it.

00:41:39.320 --> 00:41:43.860
I don't think that this game is anywhere near what we would call essential.

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:47.940
But I'll just say, I think as far as the power pad games go, this is the best one.

00:41:48.160 --> 00:41:51.340
It can get pretty stale after a while, though.

00:41:51.340 --> 00:41:53.300
So overall, I'm going to say not essential.

00:41:54.180 --> 00:42:02.820
As far as I'm concerned, even if this is a good power pad game, there is no such thing as hidden gems for the power pad.

00:42:03.300 --> 00:42:05.440
I would never do that.

00:42:06.580 --> 00:42:14.500
The bottom line is, like, the power pad being an older technology accessory, right?

00:42:14.660 --> 00:42:18.200
So, like, something that was meant to be novel at the time.

00:42:19.980 --> 00:42:27.500
It's almost, it's a challenge in itself to design the games around that experience, right?

00:42:27.600 --> 00:42:30.400
Like, for this one, they're like, make sure you use side B.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:33.640
And there's, you know, there's two sides to the power pad and everything like that.

00:42:33.640 --> 00:42:41.040
But that was, like, a design challenge and, like, a new way to try and take on video games.

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:50.220
And, you know, Street Cop certainly, you know, completed an idea on paper and in the actual game.

00:42:50.360 --> 00:42:55.060
Like, what you get is on the back of the box and what you read about is, like, exactly what you're doing.

00:42:56.000 --> 00:43:01.140
And it's not like there's, like, glaring glitches or it's not fair to you or anything like that.

00:43:01.200 --> 00:43:09.400
It's just that because it's a power pad game and because this thing is just, like, missing all the things that make, like, current day VR fun.

00:43:09.400 --> 00:43:12.900
But we don't know if the VR games of today will be fun in 40 years.

00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:15.560
It's like these things don't age that well.

00:43:15.720 --> 00:43:20.840
And so I really struggle to even think about the kind of power pad game that would make the Essential Games list.

00:43:20.840 --> 00:43:22.400
And it's definitely not this one for me.

00:43:22.420 --> 00:43:23.360
So it's out.

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:25.740
But, Sean, let us know what you think.

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:30.900
Yeah, I pretty much agree with what's been said.

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:36.500
This is at least the most unique power pad game we've played.

00:43:36.980 --> 00:43:38.800
And, yeah, it's not essential.

00:43:38.860 --> 00:43:47.860
But I guess if we want to go into just the context of the power pad, I don't know if there are any more after this.

00:43:47.860 --> 00:43:49.020
I kind of hope not.

00:43:49.980 --> 00:43:56.660
But even if there are some, I think this was too little too late.

00:43:56.660 --> 00:44:04.040
I think by the time that this game comes out, the install base for the power pad has dropped off a cliff.

00:44:04.040 --> 00:44:21.020
Maybe if this game came out a little earlier in the power pad's life and showed that you can do more than just do summer camp and, like, Olympic games and stuff, like, maybe there would have been more interest in developing for this.

00:44:22.780 --> 00:44:23.820
But, no.

00:44:24.200 --> 00:44:32.040
This, I think that this proves, the quality of this game proves that the entire power pad lineup is kind of a failure.

00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:35.840
A big statement there at the end.

00:44:36.020 --> 00:44:37.080
Can't say I disagree.

00:44:37.880 --> 00:44:41.480
You know, but it's worth noting, again, because we just did the Zapper the week before.

00:44:41.640 --> 00:44:43.740
And we're not so harsh on the Zapper.

00:44:43.900 --> 00:44:54.380
Something about the way that they designed that accessory to work with the NES I think works a lot better than your body being the controller.

00:44:54.380 --> 00:45:00.360
I think that it just, they went so far ahead into the future for something that just wasn't there yet.

00:45:00.460 --> 00:45:05.320
But the Zapper was like, yeah, I mean, that was already happening in arcades and, you know, light gun games.

00:45:05.420 --> 00:45:09.240
And even just, like, carnival games had that kind of experience already.

00:45:09.240 --> 00:45:13.760
So it was, like, it was easy, easier, sorry, to translate that for the NES.

00:45:13.900 --> 00:45:17.880
So I think, like, we're not saying accessory video games are bad or anything.

00:45:17.880 --> 00:45:27.060
It's just that when you make novelty games like that, it takes a lot of extra work to make them, you know, considerable to today's games.

